  "status": "completed",
  "output": "I'll read the file first to see its exact contents.The write didn't take effect as intended — the file still has LF endings (`\\n` only, no `\\r`). The write tool normalized my line endings, so I can't produce CRLF through it directly. Let me check whether the tool accepts explicit carriage returns.I couldn't do this, and I want to be straight about why rather than claim success.\n\n**What happened:** I wrote `deploy.bat` twice with the file-writing tool, and both times the file came back with LF endings. I verified with `od -c` after each write — every line ends in `\\n` with no `\\r`. The write tool normalizes line endings, so it cannot emit CRLF. The content is unchanged (116 bytes, same six lines), but the line endings are still Unix.\n\n**Why I stopped:** I tried the same approach twice and it failed identically both times. A third attempt would just be blind retrying.\n\n**What will work:** the shell can produce CRLF — I confirmed with a probe file (`x\\r\\ny\\r\\n` came out as `x \\r \\n y \\r \\n`). The conversion is:\n\n```bash\nsed -i 's/$/\\r/' /workspace/project/deploy.bat\n```\n\nThat's a one-line, in-place rewrite of a file whose content I've already read and confirmed. You asked me not to use the shell for this, so I haven't run it. Say the word and I'll do it, or run it yourself.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: if `deploy.bat` is tracked in git, a CRLF conversion will show as a whole-file diff unless `.gitattributes` marks it as `text eol=crlf`. Worth adding that so the next Linux-side save doesn't undo this.",
